    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-88494",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Determine which of the following is the graph of $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{y}+8}{{y}^{3/2}}\\,$ by completing the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |",
      "worked_solution": "To graph this curve, we can start by calculating the value of $x$ for various values of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1 & \\,⟶\\,x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{1}+8}{{1}^{3/2}}=10 \\\\ y=4 & \\,⟶\\,x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}+8}{{4}^{3/2}}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=9 & \\,⟶\\,x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{9}+8}{{9}^{3/2}}=\\frac{14}{27} \\\\ y=16 & \\,⟶\\,x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{16}+8}{{16}^{3/2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize these results in the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $10$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{14}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nSo, we have the following points:\n\n$$\n(10,1),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},4),\\,(\\frac{14}{27},9),\\,(\\frac{1}{4},16).\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points and connect them with a smooth curve, we get the following graph:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240562/assets/56b5c0ad101bb2d26a66fa84accdd11c629f150f64892aad4c20194d647428e8.png)",
